WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.650
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.140
Super resolution is a research field with
a ton of published papers every year, where

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:14.059
the simplest problem formulation is that we
have a low-resolution, coarse image as an

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:19.000
input and we wish to enhance it to get a crisper,
higher resolution image.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.119
You know, the thing that can always be done
immediately and perfectly in many of these

00:00:24.119 --> 00:00:26.150
detective TV series.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:32.320
And yes, sure, the whole idea of super resolution
sounds a little like…science fiction.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.530
How could I possibly get more content onto
an image that’s not already there?

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:42.160
How would an algorithm know what a blurry
text means if it’s unreadable?

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.060
It can’t just guess what somebody wrote
there, can it?

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.790
Well, let’s see.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:51.879
This paper provides an interesting take on
this topic, because it rejects the idea of

00:00:51.879 --> 00:00:54.450
having just one image as an input.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.200
You see, in this day and age, we have powerful
mobile processors in our phones, and when

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.829
we point our phone camera and take an image,
it doesn’t just take one, but a series of

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:06.079
images.

00:01:06.079 --> 00:01:10.930
Most people don’t know that some of these
images are even taken as soon as we open our

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.590
camera app without even pushing the shoot
button.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:19.500
Working with a batch of images is also the
basis of the iPhone’s beloved live photo

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.500
feature.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:25.659
So as a result, this method builds on this
raw burst input with multiple images, and

00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:30.249
doesn’t need idealized conditions to work
properly, which means that it can process

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:33.639
footage that we shoot with our shaky hands.

00:01:33.639 --> 00:01:39.429
In fact, it forges an advantage out of this
imperfection, because it can first, align

00:01:39.429 --> 00:01:45.509
these photos, and then, we have not one image,
but a bunch of images with slight changes

00:01:45.509 --> 00:01:46.509
in viewpoint.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:51.489
This means that we have more information that
we can extract from these several images,

00:01:51.489 --> 00:01:56.319
which can be stitched together into one, higher-quality
output image.

00:01:56.319 --> 00:02:02.059
Now that’s an amazing idea if I’ve ever
seen one - it not only acknowledges the limitations

00:02:02.059 --> 00:02:06.189
of real-world usage, but even takes advantage
of it.

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:07.189
Brilliant.

00:02:07.189 --> 00:02:11.039
You see throughout this video that the results
look heavenly.

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:15.810
However, not every kind of motion is desirable.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:20.170
If we have a more complex motion, such as
the one you see here as we move away from

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:24.299
the scene, this can lead to unwanted artifacts
in the reconstruction.

00:02:24.299 --> 00:02:31.170
Luckily, the method is able to detect these
cases by building a robustness mask that highlights

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:35.500
which are the regions that will likely lead
to these unwanted artifacts.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.200
Whatever is deemed to be low-quality information
in this mask is ultimately rejected, leading

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.000
to high-quality outputs even in the presence
of weird motions.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.230
And now, hold on to your papers, because this
method does not use neural networks or any

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:57.340
learning techniques, and is orders of magnitude
faster than those while providing higher-quality

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.340
images.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:04.090
As a result, the entirety of the process takes
only a 100 milliseconds to process a really

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:10.310
detailed, 12 megapixel image, which means
that it can do it 10 times every second.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.720
These are interactive framerates, and it seems
that doing this in real time is going to be

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.920
possible within the near future.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:22.530
Huge congratulations to Bart and his team
at Google for outmuscling the neural networks.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:28.159
Luckily, higher-quality ground truth data
can also be easily produced for this project,

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:31.730
creating a nice baseline to compare the results
to.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:35.859
Here you see that this new method is much
closer to this ground truth than previous

00:03:35.859 --> 00:03:36.859
techniques.

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:41.790
As an additional corollary of this solution,
the more of these jerky frames we can collect,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:47.040
the better it can reconstruct images in poor
lighting conditions, which is typically one

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.430
of the more desirable features in today’s
smartphones.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:56.819
In fact, get this, this is the method behind
Google’s magical Night Sight and Super-Res

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:01.779
Zoom features that you can access by using
their Pixel 3 flagship phones.

00:04:01.779 --> 00:04:06.599
When this feature first came out, I remember
that phone reviewers and everyone unaware

00:04:06.599 --> 00:04:11.859
of the rate of progress in computer graphics
research were absolutely floored by the results

00:04:11.859 --> 00:04:14.969
and could hardly believe their eyes when they
first tried it.

00:04:14.969 --> 00:04:19.239
And I don’t blame them, this is a truly
incredible piece of work.

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:23.669
Make sure to have a look at the paper that
contains a ton of comparisons against other

00:04:23.669 --> 00:04:29.289
methods, and it also shows the relation between
the number of collected burst frames and the

00:04:29.289 --> 00:04:33.319
output quality we can expect as a result,
and more!

00:04:33.319 --> 00:04:36.800
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

